{"subjectId":"python","id":"classes","title":"Classes & Objects","group":"Object-Oriented Python","order":31,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A class bundles data (attributes) and behaviour (methods). In Python everything — even functions and classes themselves — is an object.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Anatomy of a class</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Account:\n    bank = \"LVN Bank\"                   # class attribute: shared by all instances\n\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):\n        self.owner = owner              # instance attributes: one per object\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        if amount &lt;= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be positive\")\n        self.balance += amount\n        return self.balance\n\na = Account(\"Asha\")\nb = Account(\"Ravi\", 100)\na.deposit(50)\nprint(a.balance, b.balance, a.bank)     # 50 100 LVN Bank</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i><code>__init__</code></h3><p>The <em>initializer</em>: runs after the object is created (<code>__new__</code> creates it) to set up its state.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user\"></i><code>self</code></h3><p>The instance the method was called on. <code>a.deposit(50)</code> is really <code>Account.deposit(a, 50)</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-users\"></i>Class attribute</h3><p>Defined in the class body, shared by every instance. Great for constants and counters.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user-circle\"></i>Instance attribute</h3><p>Set on <code>self</code>, stored in the object's own <code>__dict__</code>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A mutable <strong>class</strong> attribute (e.g. <code>items = []</code> in the class body) is shared by all instances. Create per-object lists in <code>__init__</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>Three kinds of methods</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Temperature:\n    def __init__(self, celsius):\n        self.celsius = celsius\n\n    def to_f(self):                      # instance method: gets self\n        return self.celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_f(cls, f):                  # class method: gets the class → alternative constructors\n        return cls((f - 32) * 5 / 9)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_freezing(c):                  # static method: a plain function in the class namespace\n        return c &lt;= 0\n\nt = Temperature.from_f(212)\nprint(t.celsius, t.to_f(), Temperature.is_freezing(-3))   # 100.0 212.0 True</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>Properties: attributes with logic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Circle:\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self.radius = radius             # goes through the setter below\n\n    @property\n    def radius(self):\n        return self._radius\n\n    @radius.setter\n    def radius(self, value):\n        if value &lt; 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"radius must be &gt;= 0\")\n        self._radius = value\n\n    @property\n    def area(self):                      # computed, read-only\n        return round(3.14159 * self._radius ** 2, 2)\n\nc = Circle(2)\nprint(c.area)                            # 12.57\ntry:\n    c.radius = -1\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)                             # radius must be &gt;= 0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Encapsulation by convention</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Public API</td></tr><tr><td><code>_name</code></td><td>Internal — “please don't touch” (not enforced)</td></tr><tr><td><code>__name</code></td><td>Name-mangled to <code>_ClassName__name</code> to avoid clashes in subclasses</td></tr><tr><td><code>__name__</code></td><td>Special (dunder) names reserved for Python</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Python has no <code>private</code> keyword. The culture is “we're all consenting adults”: mark internals with <code>_</code> and use properties when you need validation.</p></div></section>"}